# Isolating publication from the editor

Publishing from an editor is convenient, but the editor process is the wrong place
to host Pandoc, a browser, TeX, and long-running filesystem transactions. A stuck
converter could make editing unresponsive. A large dependency graph would enlarge
the extension's trusted runtime. Cancellation could interrupt a write at exactly
the wrong moment.

Literate keeps the editor adapter small. It owns user prompts, workspace authority,
a fresh source snapshot, and a bounded request to an external helper. The helper
owns publication orchestration and returns one structured result after its output
transaction has settled.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Reader as Editor user
    participant Adapter as Editor adapter
    participant Helper as External publication helper
    participant Tools as Pandoc, browser, and TeX
    participant Store as Project filesystem

    Reader->>Adapter: Choose format, geometry, and destination
    Adapter->>Adapter: Verify trust and capture source snapshot
    Adapter->>Helper: One bounded request
    Helper->>Tools: Build through bounded subprocess adapters
    Tools-->>Helper: Bodies, resources, logs, and PDF bytes
    Helper->>Store: Commit complete output transaction
    Helper-->>Adapter: One structured result
    Adapter-->>Reader: Report output and warnings
```

The arrows show authority handoff. The helper receives a snapshot and explicit
arguments, not access to editor APIs. The diagram omits cancellation signals, which
flow from the adapter to the helper and then through each active process adapter.

## Establish authority before convenience

Publication is limited to a trusted local desktop workspace backed by ordinary
files. Remote and web workspaces are rejected. The selected workspace folder must
still belong to the current workspace at each asynchronous boundary.

The project root is resolved canonically and must be a real directory rather than a
symbolic-link alias. Source, manifest, and destination paths are checked for root
containment and safe components. A save dialog suggests a location but does not
establish authority; the returned URI is independently checked for local-file
scheme, extension, and containment.

If the selected workspace folder is removed while publication is active, authority
is revoked and cancellation is latched. Re-adding an equal-looking folder does not
restore the old operation's authority.

## Executables are explicit user choices

The helper runs under an external Node executable, never the editor's embedded
Electron process. The path is absolute and comes only from user-level settings, not
from workspace-controlled configuration. An optional Chromium path follows the
same rule.

This distinction prevents a project from silently selecting an executable merely
by changing workspace settings. Pandoc and TeX remain system dependencies checked
by the helper's normal preflight. The editor does not download tools or alter the
user's installation.

A font manifest is different: it is a root-relative project input and may be chosen
for the workspace. Its path still crosses the same containment and symlink checks as
other publication resources.

## Capture one fresh source snapshot

Publication should include unsaved literate text without forcing a save-all. The
adapter first clones every eligible open buffer synchronously. Empty and newly
created file-backed buffers override disk just like nonempty buffers. Untitled,
foreign-scheme, and out-of-root documents do not enter the snapshot.

Only after overlays are captured does the adapter traverse disk. Discovery is
sorted and excludes generated, dependency, and editor-test directories according to
the shared project policy. A disk file with an open overlay is not read a second
time.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Open[Eligible open buffers] --> Clone[Clone text before awaiting disk]
    Clone --> Overlay[Path-to-text overlays]
    Disk[Sorted project discovery] --> Choose{Overlay for path?}
    Overlay --> Choose
    Choose -->|Yes| Buffer[Use cloned buffer]
    Choose -->|No| Saved[Read bounded saved file]
    Buffer --> Snapshot[Complete source snapshot]
    Saved --> Snapshot
```

The arrows show precedence, not a merge of two texts. Every path contributes one
captured value. The diagram omits binary assets and fonts because they remain on
disk and are checked later by the helper.

The adapter accounts for serialized request bytes while collecting documents and
checks the final envelope again. Individual disk files are size-checked before
reading. This bounds memory before the helper applies its independent input bound.

## Serialize publication with other writers

A global publication guard rejects overlapping publication commands. A repository
queue, when supplied, serializes snapshot capture and the helper's complete lifetime
with ordinary generation.

Prompts occur before acquiring that queue, so a user deciding on paper or a filename
does not block unrelated work. Once acquired, the queue remains held until the
helper closes and its result is validated. A cancellation request does not release
the queue early while rollback may still be running.

This ordering gives the source snapshot and output transaction one operation
boundary. It does not claim to prevent unrelated external programs from changing
the workspace concurrently.

## One request, one helper lifetime

The helper is a one-request process. Standard input carries one JSON envelope
terminated by end-of-file. It admits only LaTeX and PDF publication commands in
structured-output mode; help, self-hosting, and unrelated command surfaces are not
available.

Input is counted as it arrives and has both a byte bound and a delivery deadline.
The helper validates the envelope before lazily loading the shared publication
runner. It writes no readiness chatter, so standard output remains a single
machine-readable result.

The implementation packages this boundary as `publication-helper.cjs`. The editor
spawns it with an argument vector and `shell: false`, sends the captured documents,
and closes standard input. The working directory is the canonical project root, but
the request still carries explicit safe relative destinations.

## Runtime bundles preserve the boundary

An installed extension carries three publication runtime artifacts:

- the external helper that owns one publication request;
- the browser worker that owns diagram rendering and Chromium; and
- the offline Mermaid program loaded by that worker.

Each artifact is built as a closed runtime graph. Unresolved package imports, source
toolchains, browser installers, and accidental development dependencies are rejected
at build time. The browser artifact has no Node imports. The helper does not expose
self-host verification or source-generation entry points that publication does not
need.

These bundles make installation independent of a development checkout while
preserving isolation. They do not bundle Pandoc, TeX, or Chromium, and they never
download those tools at runtime.

## Cancellation is graceful at the transaction boundary

The adapter forwards editor cancellation by sending a termination signal to the
helper. The helper latches cancellation before reading input and keeps signal
listeners through the shared runner's completion. Browser rendering and PDF
compilation own process groups because their tools may create descendants. The
Pandoc adapter owns and terminates its direct child only.

The editor deliberately has no automatic hard-kill fallback for the helper. Killing
it during rollback could abandon staged files or backups. If graceful termination
is slow, the adapter reports that it is waiting and keeps the publication lock until
process close.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Active
    Active --> Cancelling: user cancels or authority is revoked
    Cancelling --> Cleaning: helper acknowledges and unwinds
    Active --> Cleaning: operation completes
    Cleaning --> Closed: process closes and pipes drain
    Closed --> [*]
```

The lock follows the helper through `Cleaning`; it is not released at the
cancellation request. The diagram intentionally has no forced-termination shortcut.
Subprocess adapters may terminate their owned process groups, but the transaction
coordinator must be allowed to finish its recovery work.

## Trust the result only after protocol validation

Combined standard output and error are bounded. An execution deadline requests
helper cancellation, but it does not impose a hard upper bound on graceful cleanup.
The editor may report that cleanup is taking unusually long and continues waiting
for process close—not mere exit—because inherited pipes may still be draining and a
transaction may still be restoring files.

A zero exit status is necessary but not sufficient. The adapter parses the structured
response, checks that it describes the requested command, and requires the selected
destination to be reported as either newly written or already current. Warnings are
then displayed separately from errors.

Cancellation does not overwrite an already committed truth. If cancellation arrives
after the helper has committed and the validated response reports success, the
output is complete. If workspace authority was revoked after commit, the adapter
reports the completed path but does not remember settings, offer an open action, or
open the file.

## Prompts do not become hidden state

Paper, four margins, and destination are always explicit prompts. The previous
successful choices provide defaults for the same canonical root and output kind;
they do not bypass validation or the prompt itself. Canceling any prompt ends the
operation before a helper is spawned.

Margin validation checks both each side and the resulting body dimensions. The
renderer validates geometry again because UI checks are convenience, not a trust
boundary.

After a validated success, the editor offers **Open Output**. Only when the user
chooses it is a PDF opened through the editor's general file-open command rather
than as a text document. LaTeX and PDF destinations remain inside the selected
project so the helper's transaction and input-protection rules apply.

## Isolation keeps responsibilities honest

The editor knows about workspace trust, open buffers, prompts, progress, and user
cancellation. The helper knows about publication semantics, resources, converters,
compilers, and transactional writes. External tools receive explicit arguments and
bounded protocol streams, but they remain trusted processes with the filesystem
authority inherited from the helper. Project resources are policy-checked; not every
resource class has a byte quota. Pandoc owns one direct-child lifetime; the browser
and PDF adapters own process-group lifetimes.

No boundary is a complete sandbox. The external helper has project access, TeX is a
trusted compiler, and filesystem races with unrelated programs remain possible. The
design nevertheless prevents editor APIs, unsaved source capture, subprocess
ownership, and output commitment from collapsing into one unreviewable lifetime.

Publication therefore ends where it began: one captured literate project is
interpreted once, then carried through explicit adapters whose authority and failure
semantics match the medium they serve. With the subsystem boundaries established,
Part VII turns to concerns that cross all of them: determinism, resource limits,
contributor practice, and the limits of the system's promises.
